Ladybird sends a console.table() message in the shape that Gecko’s
DevTools server gives it in getConsoleTableMessageItems(): The first
argument’s grip gets the table’s rows in an ownProperties field, with
each row’s cells in the ownProperties field of its own grip, and a null
preview. Gecko reads those rows from the table data itself, with
enumProperties() or enumEntries(). Ladybird builds them from the rows
and columns that its console made of that data instead, as the Console
spec’s table() steps describe. V8 (ConsoleAPIType::kTable) and WebKit
(MessageType::Table) report the call’s arguments, and generate a table
preview from them for their own clients.
